{"text": "Thomas W. Barwin\n325 Forest Avenue, Oak Park, IL 60302\nCell: 708-469-9407 | tombarwin@gmail.com\nAbbreviated Resume\n\nEXPERIENCE\n\nVillage Manager - Village of Oak Park August 2006 to March 1, 2012\nCook County, Illinois\n\nManager of one of America's most innovative, diverse, and highly participative communities bordering\nChicago, with $100 million in budgets. Oak Park is home to 53,000 residents, living adjacent to 12 transit-\norientated business districts, and the Frank Lloyd Wright and Ernest Hemingway Tourist Districts.\nAccomplishments include reducing crime to a 39 year low, rebuilding the administration after a large early-\nretirement incentive program, crafting and implementing an innovative animal control and care partnership,\nreversing 10.7 million in general fund and parking fund deficits, updating housing programs, reorganizing\nperformance measurement efforts, improving economic development efforts, development and oversight of\naward winning street scape projects. Advanced Oak Park's smart grid sustainability efforts, negotiating\nseveral TOD developments, and improved municipal services in high-expectation community with a highly\nunionized workforce. Recipient of LEAN Energy \u201cGreen Breakthrough Award\u201d and 2012 nominee for the\nNational Edison Innovation Award.\n\nCity Manager - City of Ferndale March 1998 to August 2006\nOakland County, Michigan\n\nChief Administrator of inner ring Detroit suburb established in 1917 along historic Woodward Avenue.\nAchievements include acclaimed revitalization of downtown district, opening of two recreation centers,\nsecuring millions in grant funds, implementing brownfield redevelopment policies, achieving record\nnumber of new housing starts, completion of $42 million infrastructure project and $1.5 million parks\nimprovement project, and implementation of a technology master plan. Active leadership role in regional\ncoalitions addressing the environment, urban sprawl, improved regional public transportation. Founder of\nthe 23 city Michigan Suburbs Alliance representing one million residents on metro issues.\n\nCommunity Development Director - City St. Clair Shores January 1997 to March 1998\nMacomb County, Michigan\n\nAppointed by the City Manager as Department Head of the Community Development Department.\nResponsible for city planning, engineering, construction bidding/management, inspection services, grants,\nhousing and economic development in 68,000 population, 11.6 square mile city with 14 miles of waterfront.\nCoordinated planning and redevelopment of Lake St. Clair Nautical Mile and secured funding for major\nflood control project.\n\nAssistant to the City Manager - Troy November 1996 to January, 1997\nOakland County, Michigan\n\nAppointed by the City Manager to research special project initiatives, including major capital improvement\nfinancing, total quality management benchmarking, and concept development of economic revitalization\nstrategy for older area of dynamic 80,000 person, 34 square mile city.",
